1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,350
The brush finds a bowl: thin-walled, grey, carefully made, with simple designs painted on it.

2
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:13,085
Archaeologists call it Painted Grey Ware, and they find it across the plains between the Ganga and the Yamuna.

3
00:00:15,933 --> 00:00:20,808
It belongs to the centuries when the world of the Rigveda turned into something quite different.

4
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:34,670
Now we have two sources that talk to each other.

5
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:38,333
The later Vedic texts tell us what people said they were doing.

6
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:42,738
Painted Grey Ware and iron tools tell us where they were and how they lived.

7
00:00:42,738 --> 00:00:45,270
Historians use each to test the other.

8
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:53,738
The first change is iron.

9
00:00:53,738 --> 00:00:55,879
The texts call it the dark metal.

10
00:00:55,879 --> 00:01:02,100
Iron axes clear forest that copper tools could not, and iron-shod ploughs break heavier soil.

11
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:12,822
This is what lets people move off the light soils of the north-west and into the thickly wooded,

12
00:01:12,822 --> 00:01:15,684
far more fertile plains of the Ganga and the Yamuna.

13
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:22,253
So the centre of gravity moves east, into the doab.

14
00:01:22,253 --> 00:01:26,726
The Rigveda barely mentions the Ganga; later Vedic texts are full of it.

15
00:01:26,726 --> 00:01:29,293
Settlements get larger and stay put.

16
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:38,089
And once people stay put and grow a surplus, two things follow that change everything:

17
00:01:38,089 --> 00:01:42,269
land starts to matter more than cattle, and somebody starts collecting.

18
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:49,393
The chief becomes a king.

19
00:01:49,393 --> 00:01:52,150
You can watch it happen through the rituals.

20
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:54,355
The rajasuya, a royal consecration.

21
00:01:54,355 --> 00:02:02,251
The ashvamedha, the horse sacrifice, where a horse is let loose for a year and the land it crosses unchallenged is claimed.

22
00:02:07,767 --> 00:02:10,926
The vajapeya, a chariot race the king is arranged to win.

23
00:02:10,926 --> 00:02:14,958
These are enormous, expensive ceremonies, and their purpose is political:

24
00:02:14,958 --> 00:02:20,161
they turn a leader into a figure of a different order, and they need priests to run them.

25
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,062
And as the king rises, the assemblies fall.

26
00:02:28,062 --> 00:02:30,510
The samiti is mentioned less and less.

27
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:34,954
The sabha narrows into something closer to a council of notables.

28
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:42,698
Tribal units, the janas, begin to harden into territories, the janapadas,

29
00:02:42,698 --> 00:02:49,069
named after the people who hold them — and a territory is a thing a king can rule, tax and lose.

30
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:58,886
A caution, because this one catches people out.

31
00:02:58,886 --> 00:03:05,154
Painted Grey Ware is found roughly where the later Vedic texts suggest these people were, and roughly when.

32
00:03:05,154 --> 00:03:09,458
Some scholars treat that match as strong enough to identify the two.

33
00:03:16,367 --> 00:03:20,375
Others warn that a pottery style travels through trade and imitation,

34
00:03:20,375 --> 00:03:24,211
and that equating pots with peoples has misled archaeology before.

35
00:03:24,211 --> 00:03:28,405
The overlap is real; the identification is an argument, not a fact.

36
00:03:32,667 --> 00:03:35,704
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

37
00:03:35,704 --> 00:03:36,519
Iron east.

38
00:03:36,519 --> 00:03:38,449
Plough and sacrifice.

39
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:44,453
Dig Quiz.

40
00:03:44,453 --> 00:03:45,883
Pause and name it.

41
00:03:45,883 --> 00:03:52,130
First: thin grey pottery with painted designs, found across the Ganga-Yamuna doab.

42
00:03:52,130 --> 00:03:53,860
Painted Grey Ware.

43
00:03:58,533 --> 00:04:05,226
Second: a sacrifice in which a horse is let loose for a year, and the land it crosses unchallenged is claimed.

44
00:04:05,226 --> 00:04:06,490
The ashvamedha.

45
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:15,543
Third: the metal the texts call dark, which cleared the eastern forests.

46
00:04:15,543 --> 00:04:15,960
Iron.

47
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:21,406
In Mains, a frequent question asks how iron changed early Indian society.

48
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:33,349
Do not stop at better tools: go to forest clearing, surplus, settled territory, taxation and kingship.

49
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:44,216
A grey bowl takes its place in the gallery: the everyday object of the age when India acquired kings.

50
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:51,640
Next gallery: the texts themselves, and how they fit together.

51
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:55,806
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

